This confirms it. Here's the trace and the exact bug.

**Path:** `createTasks` (`src/domain/tasks.ts:294`) → `addDependency` (`src/domain/dependencies.ts:28`).

**Where the bug lives:** `createTasks`, specifically the dependency-resolution line:

```ts
specs.forEach((spec, i) => {
  for (const ref of spec.depends_on ?? []) {
    const dependsOnTaskId = tempIdToTaskId.get(ref) ?? ref;   // line 328
    addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, dependsOnTaskId);
  }
});
```

`createTasks` runs in two passes: first it creates every spec's task (building `tempIdToTaskId: temp_id → real UUID` as it goes), then it walks `depends_on` and resolves each `ref` — first checking whether it's a known `temp_id` in this batch, and only if not, falling back to treating `ref` as a literal, already-existing task id (`?? ref`).

**The trigger shape:** a spec whose own `temp_id` string is identical to the real `task_id` of some *already-existing* task that a `depends_on` entry (in that same spec, or any other spec) is trying to reference by real id.

Concretely: spec X sets `temp_id: "<existing-task-uuid>"` (reusing an existing task's real id as its own temp_id — plausible if a client mechanically reuses ids it already has on hand rather than generating fresh scratch tokens), and spec X's own `depends_on` list contains that same string, intending it as a reference to the pre-existing task, not to itself.

Walking through `createTasks`:
1. Pass 1 creates spec X's task, getting a brand-new UUID, and records `tempIdToTaskId.set("<existing-task-uuid>", <new-uuid>)`.
2. Pass 2, for spec X's `depends_on` entry `"<existing-task-uuid>"`: `tempIdToTaskId.get(ref)` succeeds (because `ref` collides with spec X's own `temp_id`), so it resolves to spec X's *own newly created* `<new-uuid>` — not the pre-existing task the client meant.
3. `addDependency(db, results[X].task_id, dependsOnTaskId)` is then called with `taskId === dependsOnTaskId === <new-uuid>`, tripping the `"A task cannot depend on itself"` check.

So the spec's `depends_on` entry never literally repeats its own *real* task id (it holds a genuine, distinct existing-task UUID) — the self-dependency is manufactured entirely by temp_id resolution taking priority over the "existing task id" fallback whenever a batch's `temp_id` happens to shadow a real id that's also referenced as a dependency.

Found it. The bug is in `createTasks` (`src/domain/tasks.ts:293-336`), specifically in the dependency-resolution loop at the end:

```ts
specs.forEach((spec, i) => {
  for (const ref of spec.depends_on ?? []) {
    const dependsOnTaskId = tempIdToTaskId.get(ref) ?? ref;
    addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, dependsOnTaskId);
  }
});
```

**How the map gets populated:** in the earlier loop, `createTasks` creates every task first, and for any spec carrying a `temp_id`, it records `tempIdToTaskId.set(spec.temp_id, task.id)` — including the mapping for the spec's own freshly-created task. Only after *all* tasks in the batch exist does it walk `depends_on` and resolve each `ref` via `tempIdToTaskId.get(ref) ?? ref` (falling back to treating `ref` as a literal existing task id if no temp_id matches).

**The trigger:** if a spec's own `depends_on` array contains a reference equal to *that same spec's own `temp_id`*, e.g.:

```json
{ "temp_id": "t1", "title": "A", "depends_on": ["t1"] }
```

then when resolving that spec's dependency, `tempIdToTaskId.get("t1")` returns the real `task.id` that was just generated for spec `t1` itself — which is also `results[i].task_id`. So `addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, dependsOnTaskId)` is called with two equal, freshly-minted UUIDs, and `addDependency` throws `"A task cannot depend on itself"`.

The client never wrote the real task id anywhere (task ids are `randomUUID()`s assigned inside `createTask`, unknowable in advance) — they only reused the same `temp_id` string twice: once declaring it, once inside that spec's own `depends_on` list. The temp_id indirection is exactly what turns an innocuous-looking client payload (no literal id repeated) into a genuine self-dependency at the resolved-id level.

## Where it happens

`createTasks` in `src/domain/tasks.ts:294-337`, specifically the dependency-resolution loop at lines 326-330:

```ts
specs.forEach((spec, i) => {
  for (const ref of spec.depends_on ?? []) {
    const dependsOnTaskId = tempIdToTaskId.get(ref) ?? ref;
    addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, dependsOnTaskId);
  }
});
```

## The trigger shape

`createTasks` creates all tasks first (assigning each spec a real, server-generated `task_id`), building a `tempIdToTaskId` map from each spec's client-chosen `temp_id` to that real id. Only afterward does it walk `depends_on` and resolve each reference against that map, falling back to treating the string as a literal existing `task_id` if it isn't a known `temp_id`.

The bug fires when a single spec's own `depends_on` array contains **that same spec's own `temp_id`** — e.g.:

```json
{ "temp_id": "T1", "title": "...", "depends_on": ["T1"], ... }
```

At the time the client writes this, `"T1"` is nothing but an arbitrary local label, and no real task id has been assigned yet — so nothing in the request "literally repeats" a task id. But once `createTask` runs for this spec, `tempIdToTaskId.set("T1", <new real id>)` is recorded. When the second pass resolves that spec's own `depends_on: ["T1"]`, it looks up `"T1"` in the map and gets back the very id that was just assigned to this same spec (`results[i].task_id`). The call becomes:

```ts
addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, results[i].task_id)
```

which hits `taskId === dependsOnTaskId` in `addDependency` (`src/domain/tasks.ts:29-31` in the dependencies module) and throws `"A task cannot depend on itself"` — surfacing as a mysterious self-dependency error even though the client's JSON never repeats one real id twice.

## What's already there

**`cancelTask`** (`src/domain/lifecycle.ts:425-459`) is the single-task primitive. Inside one `db.transaction()`, it:
- looks up the task by id and throws a plain `Error` if it's unknown, or if it's already `done`/`cancelled` ("already X, nothing to cancel")
- throws `TaskConflictError` if it's actively locked (`owner_session_id` set and `lock_expires_at` not yet expired) — unless `force: true` is passed, in which case it cancels regardless of who holds it
- sets status to `cancelled`, records `cancellation_reason`, clears `owner_session_id`/`lock_expires_at`, and returns `unblocked_task_ids` via `findUnblockedDependents` (status.ts:91), the same dependency-unblocking logic `completeTask` uses

**Listing an epic's tasks**: `requireEpic(db, projectId, epicRef)` (epics.ts:67-73) resolves/validates the epic. For the actual task set, follow `epicTaskCounts`'s pattern (status.ts:254-259) — a raw `SELECT id, status FROM tasks WHERE epic_id = ?` — rather than `listTasks`, which resolves a project ref, computes the derived `blocked` display status, and returns a lot of summary fields the bulk op doesn't need.

## New function: `cancelEpicTasks`

Add it in `lifecycle.ts` next to `cancelRelease`/`cancelTask`:

```ts
export interface CancelEpicTasksInput {
  project_id: string;
  epic_id: string;
  reason?: string;
  force?: boolean;
}
export interface CancelEpicTasksResult {
  cancelled_task_ids: string[];
  skipped: Array<{ task_id: string; status: string }>;
  unblocked_task_ids: string[];
}
```

1. `requireProject` + `requireEpic` to resolve/validate.
2. Query `SELECT id FROM tasks WHERE epic_id = ? AND status NOT IN ('done', 'cancelled')` — pre-filter so we never even attempt the ones that are obviously already finished.
3. Loop over those ids and call `cancelTask(db, { task_id, reason, force })` for each individually — **not** inside one outer all-or-nothing transaction. Each call is already its own atomic unit; wrapping the whole loop in one more transaction would mean a single conflicting task rolls back every cancellation that already succeeded, which is the opposite of what "bulk close-out" should do.
4. Catch per task: `TaskConflictError` → push `{task_id, status: 'locked'}` onto `skipped` and continue; the plain "already done/cancelled" `Error` → same (covers the race where a task got completed/cancelled by someone else between step 2's query and this call — `cancelTask`'s own status check inside its transaction still re-verifies, so that race is safe, just needs to be caught here instead of crashing the batch). Any other error, rethrow — that's a genuine bug, not an expected bulk-op outcome.
5. On success, collect the returned `unblocked_task_ids` from each call into a deduped set for the final result.

This gives every task in the epic an independent outcome: cancelled, or skipped-with-reason, and nothing about one task's state blows up the rest of the batch.

